      "id": 1,
      "text": "Bonanza Hills was originally part of the Spanish and Mexican land grant system established in the mid-eighteenth century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/bonanza_hills_texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The landscape of Bonanza Hills features thorny shrubs and small trees like mesquite and prickly pear cactus.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/bonanza_hills_texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Deep, well-drained clay loams in Bonanza Hills support local ranching but limit the possibility of intensive agriculture.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/bonanza_hills_texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Bonanza Hills experiences a semi-arid climate with hot summers and annual precipitation concentrated in summer thunderstorms.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/bonanza_hills_texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Strong ties to Mexican-American heritage in Bonanza Hills are evident through traditions like quinceañeras and Día de los Muertos.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/bonanza_hills_texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Zoned schools for the Bonanza Hills area include San Isidro Elementary, Elias Herrera Middle, and United High School.",
